# Chapter 5, part 2: the markers from Author to Equation.

.h2 Author [marker-author]
.ix Author marker
.ix Marker, Author
The Author marker prints the name of the author of the document.  Use it on a
title page or in a line such as "Prepared by" so that the name is typed once
and kept with the document.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
Source of the text | The Author field of the Save panel (File, Save), which
 is saved in the document; see {x:file-revision}.
.end
.h3 On the screen
Long mode (the default): [AUTHOR].  Name and Short: [AUTHOR].  The label never
shows the name of the author, which may be long; the name is put in at print
time.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft: the author, as text, in the font of the text around it.  A
document that has no author prints nothing for the marker (an empty text).
.h3 Edges
The marker is not a copy: it follows the Author of the document, so if the
Author is changed on the Save panel all the Author markers of the document
print the new name at the next print.  The text of the marker cannot be
changed on its own.

.h2 Column Break [marker-column-break]
.ix Column Break marker
.ix Marker, Column Break
A Column Break marker ends the page and starts the text that follows at the
top of a new page.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
Place | Best in a block of its own.
.end
.h3 On the screen
Short mode (the default): [CB].  Long: [COLUMN BREAK].  Name: [CB].
.h3 When printed
A Final or Draft print makes a page break where the marker stands: the text
after it starts on a new page.  In the cell of a table the marker is printed
as a Box (COLUMN BREAK).
.h3 Edges
- A Keep region that holds a Column Break marker is not kept: the Keep markers
  are dropped, because the page changes inside the region.
- Two Column Break markers in a row make a page break each, so a page can be
  left with only the text between them.
- See Page Break ({n:marker-page-break}) for the other marker that breaks a
  page.

.h2 Comment [marker-comment]
.ix Comment marker
.ix Marker, Comment
.ix Notes to oneself
A Comment is a piece of text that is shown on the screen and is not printed,
for notes to yourself or to the people who work on the document.  It prints
only in a Draft print.  The text can be as long as you like and is edited in a
full editor of its own.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | The text editor first (the status line shows COMMENT EDIT), then the
 panel.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Comment | The text, edited in its own editor | Empty | The text of the comment.
 Press {k:Ins} in the editor to keep it and go on to the panel, or
 {k:Esc} to cancel the whole command.
Name | Text | The name in the environment variable USER | Who wrote the comment;
 printed in a Draft print.
Created | Shown only | The time of the creation | The date and time in the
 form 2026-11-13 10:05.  It is set when the comment is made and does not
 change when the comment is edited.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-comment.png 5.4 The panel of the Comment marker, with the name of the author and the time of its creation.
If the environment variable USER is not set the Name is empty and can be typed.
The Created time is filled in when the panel opens and the field is
empty, so a comment that came from a file without a time gets the time of the
first edit.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Short, in which the label is COM "text"; in Long
mode it is COMMENT "text", where the text is the comment on one line (the ends of
lines become spaces) and is cut at about 95 characters.  Name mode shows [COM].
.h3 When printed
- Final and Preview: nothing.
- Draft: the comment is printed where it stands, in small typewriter type, as
  [[Comment by NAME, TIME: TEXT]], for example [[Comment by Ann, 2026-11-13
  10:00: Check this]].  The text is plain: the line ends become spaces and the
  attributes and blocks of the comment are not kept.
.h3 Edges
A comment that holds a block, several columns or formats shows its text on
the screen label as plain text.  A marker of a comment that has no text
prints [[Comment by NAME, TIME: ]] in a Draft print.  Search does not look into
a comment, and the Words command does not count its words.

.h2 Counter [marker-counter]
.ix Counter marker
.ix Marker, Counter
.ix Numbered lists
.ix Counter Format
.ix Roman numerals, letters as numbers
A Counter marker prints the next number of a counter.  Use it for numbered
items that are not sections: the items of a list, the figures or the tables of a
chapter, the steps of a procedure.  There are 99 counters, each with its own
count, and the look of the number (arabic, roman, letters, with a text around
it) is set once for the document and may be changed by a marker.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Counter, Set Value, Number Style.
Global settings | Counter Format, in Global, Marker: 99 counters, nine to a
 page, each with a Description and a Tag ({n:format-marker-ring}).
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Counter (1-99) | A whole number 1 to 99 | 1 | Which of the 99 counters this marker
 uses.
 Other numbers give the message Counter must be 1 to 99.
Set Value | A whole number 0 to 4294967295, or empty | Empty | Sets the counter
 to this number, and the marker prints it.  Empty: the marker adds one to the
 counter and prints that.  Any other text gives the message Set Value must be
 0 to 4294967295.
Number Style | [Style of the counter], Arabic (1), Lower Case Roman (i),
  | Upper Case Roman (I), Upper Case Letters (A),
  | Lower Case Letters (a) | [Style of the
  |  | counter] | The style of the number.  The first entry takes the style
 of the counter's tag.  Roman numerals show 1 to 3999 and the letters
 show 1 and more: a number that the style cannot show (0, or above 3999 in
 roman) is written in arabic digits.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-counter.png 5.4 The panel of the Counter marker: counter 1, the value set to 5, the style Lower Case Roman.
.h3 How the numbers are counted
The counters are counted in the order of the text of the document (the blocks
one after another, and in a block the columns from left to right); they have
nothing to do with pages.  For each counter:
1. The first marker of the counter in the document prints its Set Value if it
   has one, and 1 if it has not.
2. Every later marker adds one to the counter and prints that, unless it has a
   Set Value, which sets the counter to that value.  So Set Value 7 makes
   the numbers go on 7, 8, 9 and so on; a Set Value of 1 starts again from 1.
   Each counter counts up to 4294967295, the largest number that fits in 32
   bits, and stops there: a marker after the counter has reached it prints
   4294967295 again, and the counter does not go back to 0.  The Printer
   reports this once in its log (the warning counter-limit).
3. A style that a marker chooses stays with its counter from that marker on,
   until another marker chooses another style.  Choosing the first entry
   [Style of the counter] does not undo it.
The counters are independent: items counted by counter 1 and counter 2 in the
same list print 1. 2. 3. and A. B. C. each in its own count.
.h3 The tag
The tag of a counter, which is set in Counter Format, is the text that the
marker prints, with the number in it.  The tag has at most 90 characters.  It
is made of ordinary characters and one style letter between backslashes, which
is one of the characters in this table.
.table 6 54
Code | The number is written as
\1\ | Arabic: 1, 2, 3.
\i\ | Lower case roman numerals: i, ii, iii, iv.
\I\ | Upper case roman numerals: I, II, III, IV.
\A\ | Upper case letters: A, B, ..., Z, AA, AB, ...
\a\ | Lower case letters: a, b, ..., z, aa, ab, ...
.end
The first code in the tag stands for the number and the rest of the tag is
kept as it is: the tag "Table \I\." prints Table I., Table II., and
the tag "\1\)" prints 1), 2).  A tag with no code gets the number at its
start.  A second code in the same tag is printed as it is.  The tags that a
new document has are "\1\." for every counter except counter 2, which has "\A\."
(a counter that has no tag of its own uses these).  The Number Style of
a marker replaces the style of the tag and keeps the rest of the tag: a marker
with Upper Case Roman Numerals and the tag "\1\." prints III.
.h3 Edges
- A number that the style cannot show is written in arabic: a roman numeral
  needs a number from 1 to 3999 and a letter style needs a number of 1 or
  more.
- The Description of a counter, also set in Counter Format, is a comment for
  the reader.  It is never printed; it is added to the label of the marker on
  the screen in the Short mode: C1, list of books.
- Counters in the text of a note or a comment are not counted.

.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Long: the marker is drawn as its text, such as 3., without
brackets and in the color of markers, so a list of counters looks like a list
of numbers.  Short: [C1] (with the description if there is one, as in [C1, list
of books]).  Name: [C].  Diamond and Hidden as usual.  The numbers on the
screen follow every change of the text.
.h3 When printed
Final and Draft: the text of the counter, in the font of the text around it.  It
is printed in every display mode (the mode does not hide it).
.h3 Messages
- Counter must be 1 to 99: the Counter field is not a whole number from 1 to
  99.
- Set Value must be 0 to 4294967295: the field holds something that is not a
  whole number in that range (a sign, a decimal point and letters are not
  allowed).

.h2 Cross-reference [marker-cross-reference]
.ix Cross-reference marker
.ix Marker, Cross-reference
.ix Section numbers in the text
.ix Refer to a section
A Cross-reference prints the tag, the headline, both, or the page number of
another section of the document.  Use it instead of typing "see section 3.2",
so that the reference stays right when sections are added, deleted or moved.
It can only be used in a structured document.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Section Number, Style, and the shown values Section Tag and Headline.
Place | Structured documents only.  In an unstructured document the name is in
 parentheses on the Marker Commands table and the command gives Invalid marker
 selection.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Section Number | The number of a section as the status line shows it, such as
  | 1, 3.1 or 2.4.1 | None (required) | The section that is referred to.  A
 number that is not a section gives Cannot find section number.
Style | [Tag], Tag, Headline, Both, Page Number | [Tag] | What is printed (the
 table below).
Section Tag | Shown only | | The tag of the section that the number names.
Headline | Shown only | | The headline of that section.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-xref.png 5.4 The panel of the Cross-reference marker for the section 1.2: the tag and the headline of the section are shown.
The Section Number is always the decimal number that the status line shows
(1.2.1), whatever the section tag looks like: a section whose tag is "Part II"
is still number 2 here.  The two shown fields change when the number is typed
and let you see that the right section was found before you press {k:Ins}.
.h3 The styles
.table 12 48
Style | What is printed
[Tag] | What the Cross-reference Format of the Global menu says: the tag, the
 headline or both (the tag when the format is not set).  The marker follows the
 format when it is changed later ({n:format-marker-format}).
Tag | The tag of the section, such as 2.1.  If the section has no tag (the
 Section Tag of its level is empty or Auto Numbering gives it none) the number
 is printed instead.
Headline | The text of the headline, as plain text on one line (the first 160
 characters).
Both | The tag, a space and the headline.
Page Number | The number of the page on which the section starts.
.end
.h3 How the marker follows its section
The marker keeps the section it was made for.  When a section is added,
deleted, moved, promoted or sorted, the marker follows that section, and the
number in the marker is changed to the new number of the section; the document
counts as changed when a number moved.  If the section is deleted the marker
prints [no section] and keeps its last number.  When a document is opened,
the marker refers to the section that has the number it shows.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Long: the marker is drawn as the text that it prints
(for the style Page Number the words "page of" and the tag), without brackets.
Short: [XREF 1.1].  Name: [XREF].  Diamond: a diamond.
.h3 When printed
The text of the style.  The headline, the tag and the number are typeset in
the font of the text around the marker.
.h3 Edges
- A cross-reference to the section it is in is allowed.
- The headline is taken from the first column of the headline block, as plain
  text: attributes are lost.
- With the Page Number style the number is the page on which the headline of
  the section stands.
.h3 Messages
- Cannot find section number: the number is not that of a section.
- Invalid marker selection (Not available in an unstructured document).

.h2 Date [marker-date]
.ix Date marker
.ix Marker, Date
.ix Date format
.ix Month, Mon, Year, YY, MM, DD
A Date marker prints the date on which the document is printed.  Use it for
the date of a letter or of a report so that it is always the date of the print.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | Format.
Global setting | Date Format on the Marker Format panel of Global, Marker.
.end
.h3 The panel
.table 11 15 9 22
Field | Values | Default | Effect
Format | Text made of the date words below and ordinary characters |
  |  | Month D, Year (or the Date Format of the document) | How the date is
 written.
.end
.pic pictures/markers-date.png 5.4 The panel of the Date marker, with the format Mon D, Year.
The words are not case sensitive.  Anything else in the format is printed as
it is.
.table 6 54
Word | Meaning
Month | The name of the month in full: November.
Mon | The first three letters of the name: Nov.
Year, YYYY | The year with four digits: 2026.
YY | The year with two digits: 26.
MM | The month as two digits: 11.
M | The month as a number without a zero in front: 11.
DD | The day of the month as two digits: 07.
D | The day of the month: 7.
.end
.table 20 30
Format | Gives (13 November 2026)
Month D, Year | November 13, 2026
M/D/YY | 11/13/26
DD Mon YYYY | 13 Nov 2026
YYYY-MM-DD | 2026-11-13
.end
.caution Every letter D, M or Y in the format is a date word, wherever it
  stands.  The format "Today is Month D" prints "To13ay is November 13",
  because the d of "Today" is the day.  Words in a format must not have those
  letters: a text of your own is better put outside the marker.
.h3 On the screen
Long and Short: the date of today in brackets, such as [November 13, 2026].
Name: [DATE].
.h3 When printed
The date at the time of the print, as text.  The month names are in English.
The Printer prints the date according to the display mode of the Date marker:
see {x:markers-print-modes}.
.h3 Edges
- A format that is left as the default follows the Date Format of the Global
  menu.
- The format cannot hold an empty string: an empty field is the default.

.h2 Description [marker-description]
.ix Description marker
.ix Marker, Description
The Description marker prints the description of the document, the text that was
typed in the Description field of the Save panel.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | None.  The marker is inserted at once.
Source of the text | The Description field of the Save panel; see
 {x:file-revision}.
.end
.h3 On the screen
Long: [DESCRIPTION].  Short and Name: [DESC].
.h3 When printed
The description as text.
.h3 Edges
- A document without a description prints nothing.

.h2 Equation [marker-equation]
.ix Equation marker
.ix Marker, Equation
.ix Formulas
An Equation marker holds a formula that is typeset as mathematics.  The formula
is written in TeX math, as {x:equations} describes.
.table 12 48
Item | Value
Panel | The text editor only (the status line shows EQUATION EDIT); there are no
 other fields.
.end
.h3 How it is entered
{k:Alt-F12}, Equation opens a full editor for the formula.  Type the formula
as TeX math.  {k:Ins} saves it and inserts the marker, {k:Esc} throws it away
and {k:Ctrl-W} shows the formula as it looks.  To change it, put the cursor
on the marker and press {k:Alt-F12}: the editor opens with the formula.
.h3 On the screen
The default mode is Inline: in the X11 version the formula is shown as the
picture that LaTeX makes of it; in the terminal version, and while the picture
is not ready, the label.  The other modes show EQN, EQN x^2 (Short, the formula
on one line) and EQUATION x^2 (Long).
.h3 When printed
The formula is typeset as mathematics.  A formula that is alone on its line
(only spaces or attributes are beside it) is printed as a display equation,
centered on a line of its own; in any other place it is printed inline, in the
line of text.  In the cell of a table it is always inline.  The ends of lines in
the formula become spaces.
.h3 Edges
- A formula that TeX could not typeset is printed as a Box: EQUATION followed by
  the formula.  A formula is rejected before a run of TeX is made: an empty
  one, one
  longer than 4000 characters, one with a character outside plain ASCII, one
  with the characters %, # or $, braces that do not match, a begin or end of
  an environment that is not on its list, or a command that can write files or
  run programs.  The rest is tried, and the ones that TeX does not accept are
  boxed.
- The Search command does not look into the formula.
